CAT = "pkpnmcFund"; SEG = "FUN"
Q = [
("Where is the stethoscope placed to count an adult's apical pulse?",
 "Fifth left intercostal space, mid-clavicle", ["Second intercostal space, right of sternum", "Over the left collarbone, near the neck", "Just below the right nipple, at the side"],
 "The apex of the heart lies at about the fifth left intercostal space in the midclavicular line, so the apical beat is heard best there. It is counted for a full minute."),
("A patient had surgery inside the mouth this morning. Which route should the nurse avoid when taking a temperature?",
 "Oral", ["Axillary", "Tympanic", "Temporal artery"],
 "An oral thermometer could injure the operated tissue and the reading may be wrong. Axillary or tympanic readings are used instead."),
("Which solution is usually used to clean a spill of blood on a ward floor?",
 "Diluted hypochlorite (bleach)", ["Plain soap and cold water", "Surgical spirit, then a dry cloth", "Very hot water with no cleaner added"],
 "Chlorine-releasing solutions such as diluted hypochlorite kill blood-borne viruses on hard surfaces. The nurse wears gloves and an apron, soaks up the spill and follows the hospital's spill procedure."),
("Which gloves does a nurse wear to empty a patient's urine drainage bag?",
 "Clean non-sterile gloves", ["Sterile surgical gloves", "No gloves, hands only", "Heavy rubber kitchen gloves"],
 "Emptying a drainage bag is a dirty task with a splash risk, so clean examination gloves and an apron are worn. Sterile gloves are kept for aseptic procedures."),
("Over the sacrum, the skin is intact but red, and the redness does not fade when pressed. What stage of pressure injury is this?",
 "Stage 1", ["Stage 2", "Stage 3", "Unstageable"],
 "Non-blanchable redness of intact skin is stage 1. Stage 2 has partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis, such as a shallow open area or blister."),
("What does the Braden scale help the nurse assess?",
 "Risk of pressure injury", ["Risk of falling out of bed", "Depth of unconsciousness", "Severity of pain"],
 "The Braden scale scores sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition and friction or shear. A lower score means a higher risk of pressure injury."),
("Unless there is a reason not to, why is the head of the bed kept at 30 degrees or lower for a patient at risk of pressure injury?",
 "To reduce sliding and shear", ["To improve the patient's appetite", "To keep the patient awake", "To make bed baths faster"],
 "When the head of the bed is raised high, the patient slides down and the skin over the sacrum is dragged against deeper tissue. This shear damages small vessels and causes pressure injury."),
("Which bedside check best confirms that a nasogastric tube is in the stomach before a feed?",
 "An acidic pH on testing the aspirate", ["Bubbles with the end in water", "A whoosh of air on the stethoscope", "The patient can still talk"],
 "Stomach contents are acidic, so a low pH on indicator paper supports gastric placement. Local policy sets the cut-off. The air whoosh test is unreliable, and an X-ray is the most reliable check when in doubt."),
("A patient with COPD who is at risk of carbon dioxide retention needs oxygen. Which saturation target is usually set until blood gas results are known?",
 "88 to 92 percent", ["94 to 98 percent", "Exactly 100 percent", "75 to 80 percent"],
 "Too much oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention in these patients, so a lower target of 88 to 92 percent is used until a blood gas guides treatment."),
("What is used to inflate the balloon of an indwelling urinary catheter?",
 "Sterile water", ["Tap water from the ward", "Room air from a syringe", "Dextrose 5%"],
 "Manufacturers specify sterile water in the volume printed on the catheter. Air lets the balloon float and tap water is not sterile."),
("In which position is a woman usually placed for insertion of a urinary catheter?",
 "Dorsal recumbent, knees bent apart", ["Prone with a pillow under the hips", "High Fowler's with legs straight", "Right lateral with knees together"],
 "Lying on the back with the knees bent and apart exposes the urethral meatus, so the catheter can be passed with aseptic technique."),
("A patient stands up and the systolic pressure falls by 25 mmHg within 3 minutes. What is this called?",
 "Orthostatic hypotension", ["White coat hypertension", "Pulse deficit", "Essential hypertension"],
 "A fall of 20 mmHg or more in systolic pressure, or 10 mmHg or more in diastolic, within 3 minutes of standing is orthostatic hypotension. The patient is at risk of falling."),
("What is the normal range for the specific gravity of urine?",
 "1.005 to 1.030", ["0.900 to 0.950", "1.100 to 1.200", "2.000 to 2.500"],
 "Specific gravity shows how concentrated urine is. A high value suggests dehydration, and a fixed low value can suggest the kidneys cannot concentrate urine."),
("For most acutely ill adults with no risk of carbon dioxide retention, what is the usual oxygen saturation target?",
 "94 to 98 percent", ["88 to 92 percent", "80 to 85 percent", "Exactly 100 percent"],
 "94 to 98 percent is the usual target for acutely ill adults. A lower target is kept for patients at risk of carbon dioxide retention, such as many with COPD."),
("A nurse forgot to record a dressing change and remembers two hours later. How should she write it in the notes?",
 "As a late entry, timed now", ["Squeezed into the earlier space", "On a separate loose sheet of paper", "As if written at the time of care"],
 "A late entry is labelled as such, with the current date and time and the time the care was given. Back-dating or squeezing in an entry can look like falsifying a legal record."),
("Which measure most reduces the risk of a fall for a confused older patient on the ward?",
 "Bed at its lowest height", ["Raising all four side rails", "Dimming lights fully at night", "Keeping the call bell out of reach"],
 "A low bed with brakes on reduces the distance of a fall. Climbing over raised rails can cause worse injuries, and the call bell should always be within reach."),
("When should a sharps container be closed and sent for disposal?",
 "When it is three-quarters full", ["Only when it is overflowing", "As soon as the first needle goes in", "At the end of every month"],
 "Overfilled containers cause needlestick injuries when more sharps are pushed in. The box is locked once it reaches the fill line, about three-quarters full."),
("Which muscle is the usual site for an injected vaccine in a baby under one year?",
 "Vastus lateralis (outer thigh)", ["Deltoid (upper arm)", "Dorsogluteal (buttock)", "Rectus abdominis (front of the belly)"],
 "The outer thigh muscle is the largest and best developed in infants. The deltoid is too small until later, and the buttock risks injury to the sciatic nerve."),
("Why does a nurse use the Z-track technique for some intramuscular injections?",
 "To stop the drug leaking back", ["To make the injection painless", "To reach a vein faster", "To avoid needing to aspirate"],
 "Pulling the skin to one side before injecting and releasing it after leaves a zigzag track that seals the drug in the muscle, which matters for irritant drugs such as iron."),
("Why is the ventrogluteal site often preferred over the dorsogluteal site for adult intramuscular injections?",
 "It is far from major nerves", ["It has the thinnest muscle", "It hurts more, so it is felt", "It needs the shortest needle"],
 "The ventrogluteal site has thick muscle and lies away from the sciatic nerve and large blood vessels that run near the dorsogluteal site."),
("Why is a patient watched closely during the first 15 minutes of a blood transfusion?",
 "Severe reactions start early", ["The bag is most likely to burst", "The drip rate cannot be set before", "Blood clots form only at the start"],
 "Acute haemolytic and anaphylactic reactions often begin within minutes, so the nurse checks vital signs at the start and stays close for the first 15 minutes."),
("Before a unit of blood is hung, what do two staff check together at the bedside?",
 "Patient ID against the blood unit", ["The ward's daily cleaning list", "The patient's food preferences and diet plan", "The time of the last meal"],
 "The patient's name, hospital number and blood group are checked against the blood unit and the compatibility label, aloud and at the bedside, to prevent giving the wrong blood."),
("Once a unit of red cells has been taken out of the blood fridge, within how long should the transfusion be finished?",
 "Within 4 hours", ["Within 30 minutes", "Within 12 hours", "Within 24 hours"],
 "Red cells warm up outside the fridge and bacteria can multiply, so the unit is started promptly and completed within 4 hours."),
("Using Maslow's hierarchy, which need does the nurse meet first?",
 "Airway and breathing", ["Self-esteem and respect", "Love and a sense of belonging", "Self-actualisation"],
 "Physiological needs such as breathing, circulation, food and fluids come before safety, belonging, esteem and self-actualisation."),
("A patient says, \"My surgery is tomorrow and I can't sleep.\" Which reply reflects the patient's feelings?",
 "You sound worried about tomorrow.", ["Everyone sleeps badly in hospital.", "I will ask for a sleeping tablet.", "Don't worry, the surgeon is good."],
 "Reflecting names the feeling behind the words, showing the nurse has listened and inviting the patient to say more. False reassurance and changing the subject close the conversation."),
("How is the skin around a urinary catheter usually cleaned in routine care?",
 "Soap and water, away from the meatus", ["Antiseptic powder twice a day", "Alcohol swabs every hour, day and night", "It is never cleaned while in place"],
 "Daily washing with soap and water, from the meatus outwards and along the catheter, keeps the area clean. Strong antiseptics are not needed for routine care."),
("A patient has suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Which protective item should a nurse wear when entering the room?",
 "An N95 respirator", ["A surgical mask only", "Gloves only", "A plastic apron only"],
 "TB spreads by airborne droplet nuclei. A fitted N95 or similar respirator filters these fine particles, which a surgical mask does not reliably do."),
("Which item is cleaned or changed between patients to stop the spread of infection by contact?",
 "The shared stethoscope", ["The patient's own toothbrush", "The ward clock", "The nurse's name badge clip"],
 "Shared equipment that touches skin, such as stethoscopes and blood pressure cuffs, can carry organisms from one patient to the next, so it is wiped with disinfectant between patients."),
]
